tessera (0.5.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  A running tesserad is not restarted by the upgrade: restart it when an
  audio interruption is acceptable. Upgrade tessera and tessera-web
  together.

  With the native device role, a controller is now told the device latency
  range (from latency_bounds_us and the platform's timing floor) and may
  write the configured or the active latency. Nothing to do on upgrade.

  tessera-web: the rate follower ([appliance.rate_follow], off by
  default) makes the tessera device's sample rate the only rate of a
  speaker whose sources share a dmix bus in front of CamillaDSP. The
  package creates /var/lib/tessera-rate/alsa.conf (tessera.rate 48000);
  the bus and the bridge need configuration changes before it is enabled:
  docs/raspberry-pi-speaker.md, "Shared sources and one sample rate".

 -- tessera maintainers <tessera@invalid>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

tessera (0.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  A running tesserad is not restarted by the upgrade: restart it when an
  audio interruption is acceptable. Upgrade tessera, tessera-alsa,
  tessera-web and tessera-dsp together (and tessera-pw if installed).

  The daemon can serve the device role itself (the native device role,
  opt-in). Nothing switches on upgrade: tessera-controller@main stays
  enabled, and a disabled one stays disabled. To switch a host, stop and
  disable tessera-controller@main (both bind the same ports), then add a
  tesserad.service drop-in with Environment=TESSERA_NATIVE_DEVICE=true and
  Environment=TESSERA_NATIVE_VENDOR=<the --vendor value of
  /usr/lib/tessera/controller.args>, run systemctl daemon-reload and
  restart tesserad. Steps and the way back: docs/interoperability.md,
  "Native device role".

  PTPv1 changes take effect on every host when tesserad restarts: a
  leader-capable port advertises stratum 0x2A to 0x2D instead of 4, Sync is
  sent every 250 ms instead of every second, and general messages use DSCP
  46 (EF) instead of 8; review switch QoS rules that matched the old value.
  Clock role follower and Preferred Leader (priority below 64) now exclude
  each other; a stored or configured combination is corrected at start
  with a warning (the follower role wins).

  New: the portal's Routing page and tesseractl routing; the device access
  lock (tesseractl access, the portal's Lock and Unlock; a vendor controller
  sees it but cannot set it); [control] allow_factory_reset, default false,
  which keeps factory reset and clear configuration refused.

  The deprecated 90-probe-flows.toml fallback is still read; its removal
  remains deferred.

 -- tessera maintainers <tessera@invalid>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

tessera (0.4.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  This is a development checkpoint. The Device page can browse sources and
  edit saved subscription intent; it does not establish or remove audio flows.
  Browser Connect/Replace/Disconnect and selected-source audio/continuity
  acceptance remain unfinished. Private routing diagnostics are disabled by
  default; no native routing dispatcher is enabled.

  Network device browsing is opt-in (discovery.browse_devices = true).
  The deprecated 90-probe-flows.toml fallback is retained in this version;
  its previously announced 0.4.0 removal is deferred.

 -- tessera maintainers <tessera@invalid>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

tessera (0.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  Upgrading from 0.2.2 or 0.2.1. A running tesserad is not restarted by the
  upgrade: restart it when an audio interruption is acceptable to run the new
  binary. Upgrade tessera, tessera-alsa, tessera-web and tessera-dsp together
  (and tessera-pw if installed); the packages depend on each other's exact
  version.

  The add-on bundle changed. install.sh and uninstall.sh are gone: the
  archive carries one program, tessera-bundle, with the same options
  (tessera-bundle install --profile speaker --audio-card hw:CARD=...,
  tessera-bundle uninstall --purge) and a new verify command. It
  authenticates the release first. This release ships no trusted key: put the
  minisign public key of the release in the root-owned file
  /etc/tessera/trusted-release-keys (mode 0644, one bare key per line;
  docs/bundle.md), or, until you have one, install with --allow-unsigned,
  which is meant for development builds. Without either, the install stops
  with "untrusted_key" and changes nothing. The record of an earlier
  installation (/var/lib/tessera-bundle/record.json) is read as it is.

  Speakers with CamillaDSP (0.2.2): nothing changes in your files. The
  installer never rewrites the device configuration, the portal's
  /etc/tessera/web.d/60-speaker.toml, the CamillaDSP configurations or the
  state file. Restart camilladsp and camillagui when convenient. The
  Speaker page now warns when the EQ engine's capture rate differs from the
  device's rate, and can fix it. The portal now saves the amplifier volume
  itself. The unit drop-in written by an earlier installer
  (/etc/systemd/system/tessera-web.service.d/60-speaker.conf) is not
  rewritten: to let the Volume pane follow the sound card when its index
  changes, replace its DeviceAllow=/dev/snd/controlC<n> line with the device
  lines of /usr/share/doc/tessera-web/examples/appliance-paths.conf, remove
  ReadWritePaths=-/var/lib/alsa and restart tessera-web.

  Tessera-only speakers (0.2.1, or 0.2.2 installed with --no-dsp): running
  tessera-bundle install --profile speaker again converts the speaker to the
  CamillaDSP speaker in place (see the 0.2.2 entry); add --no-dsp to keep it
  as it is. A [[bridge]] that is not the profile's own makes the install
  refuse before anything changes. CamillaGUI (port 5005) has no login: it
  listens on 127.0.0.1 unless the bundle installer set the speaker's
  management address (a GUI that listened on every interface is narrowed on
  upgrade; a speaker that had no GUI does not get one). A speaker with a
  CamillaDSP of its own keeps its program, configurations and portal services;
  its unit file is moved aside (camilladsp.service.pre-tessera-dsp) so the
  packaged unit takes over.

  Controller flows are now stored in state.toml and applied over the control
  socket; tesseractl flows show|set|clear manages them. An existing
  /etc/tessera/config.d/90-probe-flows.toml is still read, with a warning,
  until a flow set has been stored; support for the file is removed in 0.4.0.

  Network documents gain the optional field dns_search (DNS search domains of
  the management network). A tessera-network or NDIMon-R older than this
  release rejects a document that carries search domains: upgrade both before
  you configure any. The Network page also drives NetworkManager and ifupdown
  now; those two backends are new and not yet accepted on live hosts.

  Speakers with CamillaDSP: the engine is now linked into tesserad's wants
  (WantedBy=tesserad.service), so a network change, which stops and starts
  the daemon, no longer leaves the engine stopped and the speaker silent.
  The upgrade enables the engine again, which creates the link; an engine
  switched off stays off. The portal restarts itself when a sound card
  appears while it runs (a udev rule and tessera-web-sound.service), so the
  Volume pane finds a USB card plugged in later; sign in again afterwards.

 -- tessera maintainers <tessera@invalid>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

tessera (0.2.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  The speaker profile of the add-on bundle now includes the CamillaDSP stage
  (package tessera-dsp) by default: CamillaDSP plays the network audio to the
  card and the portal's Speaker page controls it. Use tessera-bundle install
  --no-dsp for the previous tessera-only speaker with the in-daemon bridge.
  Changes made from a network controller that restart a device are now
  applied (they were refused before). Local portal settings can be kept in
  /etc/tessera/web.d/.

 -- tessera maintainers <tessera@invalid>  Sun, 25 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

tessera (0.2.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  Shipping defaults now include and enable the Controller compatibility
  runtime with the daemon, and enable device advertisement. The portal and
  its Services page are included in bundles. Fresh portal installations use
  password tessera; existing password files are never replaced. The portal's
  service user joins tessera-admin so authenticated settings controls work
  without a manual membership step. Existing service lists are retained.
  Old manually installed username-based Controller instances migrate to
  @main for the next deliberate daemon restart; installation does not stop
  the running legacy helper.
  A local /etc/systemd/system/tessera-controller@.service override is kept
  and prevents automatic migration. Review/remove it before switching to
  the packaged service. Retired/unsupported device keys do not retry.

  Media scheduling now uses the raw hardware oscillator independently of
  NTP adjustments. The local clock overlay is layout 2.0: upgrade and restart
  the daemon, ALSA clients and PipeWire clients together.

 -- tessera maintainers <tessera@invalid>  Thu, 22 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

tessera (0.2.0~beta-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  Tessera 0.2.0-beta adds standalone management/audio VLAN configuration with
  recoverable apply in the web portal. NDIMon remains the network owner when
  it hosts Tessera; configure that appliance through NDIMon instead.

  Back up /etc/tessera and /var/lib/tessera before upgrading. A running audio
  daemon is not silently restarted by package installation. Restart it when
  an audio interruption is acceptable to activate the new binary.

  The following configuration migration still applies to older installations:

  /etc/tessera/config.toml is now a reference file: every configuration key,
  commented out at its default, with a short explanation. It used to be an
  active sample. Your own settings belong in /etc/tessera/config.d/*.toml,
  which is read after config.toml and which the package never touches.

  IF YOU EDITED /etc/tessera/config.toml: move your settings into a file in
  /etc/tessera/config.d/ (for example 50-site.toml) BEFORE you take the new
  version at the dpkg prompt. Then it does not matter whether you take the
  new reference or keep your file (unattended upgrades with --force-confold
  keep it). To read the reference of the installed version, run
  "tesserad --print-default-config"; a copy is also installed as
  /usr/share/doc/tessera/examples/config.toml, but that file is absent on
  images that strip /usr/share/doc.

  If you never edited it, the upgrade replaces it silently and the daemon
  behaves as before, with one exception: the old sample defined the device
  "main" (a [[device]] entry with id = "main"). The daemon creates that
  device by itself only while no file defines a device. If a drop-in in
  config.d/ adds another device (id = "b") and you relied on the sample's
  "main", then after the upgrade only "b" exists, and its default name loses
  the "-b" suffix (a device name is visible on the network). Add a
  [[device]] entry with id = "main" to the drop-in to keep the old device.
  Check with: tesseractl config show

 -- tessera maintainers <tessera@invalid>  Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
